# RISC0 Guest Program Standards

## Context
- Applied when implementing RISC0 v1.2.1 guest programs
- Focuses on correct program structure and output formatting
- Ensures consistent proof generation patterns
- See 2001-risc0-api for high-level RISC0 integration guidelines
- See 2002-risc0-types for import standards

## Requirements
- Follow guest program structure:
  - Use `#![no_main]` and `#![no_std]` when not using std feature
  - Use `risc0_zkvm::guest::entry!(main)` for entry point
  - Import guest-specific types from `risc0_zkvm::guest`
  - Use correct digest types from `risc0_zkvm::guest::sha`
- Handle inputs and outputs correctly:
  - Read private inputs using `env::read()`
  - Commit outputs in structured format
  - Use consistent markers for output sections
  - Hash inputs for verification when needed
- Follow output formatting standards:
  - Use marker bytes to separate output sections
  - Structure binary outputs for efficient verification
  - Include human-readable outputs when helpful
  - Maintain consistent byte ordering
- Implement proper error handling:
  - Use `Result` types for fallible operations
  - Handle all error cases explicitly
  - Provide meaningful error messages
  - Avoid panics in production code

## Examples

<example>
// Good: Basic guest program structure
#![no_main]
#![no_std]

use risc0_zkvm::guest::{env, sha};

risc0_zkvm::guest::entry!(main);

fn main() {
    // 1. Read private inputs
    let input: Vec<u8> = env::read();
    
    // 2. Perform computation
    let result = compute(&input);
    
    // 3. Commit outputs in structured format
    // a. Hash of input for verification
    let input_hash = sha::digest(&input);
    env::commit(&input_hash);
    
    // b. Structured output with marker
    let mut output = vec![0xAA]; // Marker byte
    output.extend(result);
    env::commit(&output);
}
</example>

<example>
// Good: Error handling in guest program
use risc0_zkvm::guest::{env, sha};

fn process_input(input: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, &'static str> {
    if input.is_empty() {
        return Err("Input cannot be empty");
    }
    
    let result = compute(input)?;
    Ok(result)
}

fn main() {
    let input: Vec<u8> = env::read();
    match process_input(&input) {
        Ok(result) => {
            env::commit(&result);
        }
        Err(e) => {
            // Commit error state for verification
            env::commit(&[0xFF]); // Error marker
            env::commit(e.as_bytes());
        }
    }
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Incorrect program structure
fn main() { // Missing #![no_main] and entry macro
    let input = std::fs::read("input.txt").unwrap(); // Wrong: using std
    println!("Result: {:?}", result); // Wrong: using println
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Unsafe output handling
fn main() {
    let result = compute();
    env::commit(&result); // Missing structure/markers
    // No input verification
    // No error handling
}
</example>

## Critical Notes
<critical>
- ALWAYS use proper entry point macro
- NEVER use std without explicit feature flag
- Structure outputs consistently
- Handle all error cases explicitly
- Include input verification when needed
</critical> 